(504175, 48215, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Hi WildBlue...<br/> <br/>Cute pooch here :)  He doesn''t look particularly surprised though... I think you may have suffered in the challenge because of that.<br/> <br/>In the future, you should include your exposure settings and your own thoughts and ideas in the photographer''s comments box if you want to request CC critiques :)<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Hi WildBlue... Cute pooch here :)  He doesn''t look particularly surprised though... I think you may have suffered in the challenge because of that. In the future, you should include your exposure settings and your own thoughts and ideas in the photographer''s comments box if you want to request CC critiques :)', '2003-12-04 22:26:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(504178, 48202, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Hi Jon...<br/> <br/>I think I summed up my opinion on this photo in my original comment.  The composition and idea behind this photograph are both excellent.  It''s just something about the hands looking dirty to me that makes this photo fall.  It makes it feel cold and harsh instead of warm and loving, which, by the title, would be my derivation of the theme you are presenting here.  It creates a bit of internal conflict in your image.<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Hi Jon... I think I summed up my opinion on this photo in my original comment.  The composition and idea behind this photograph are both excellent.  It''s just something about the hands looking dirty to me that makes this photo fall.  It makes it feel cold and harsh instead of warm and loving, which, by the title, would be my derivation of the theme you are presenting here.  It creates a bit of internal conflict in your image.', '2003-12-04 22:28:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(504245, 48308, 7687, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>Well, I have to say that this shot does have that "surprise" look to it.  It makes me wish you had included some information in the comments eluding to what she was doing to look so surprised. :)<br/> <br/>I think some commenters are confusing focus with motion blur.  The part of the exposure that was caught by the flash is certainly in focus.  What they''re referring to is the motion blur caused by a combination of ambient light and a longer shutter speed.  At any rate, I think this movement helps emphasize the element of "surprise" in that, thanks to the motion blur, we can see that she''s actually in the process of turning around/being caught.<br/> <br/>People have mentioned the red eye, and that is an unfortunate side effect of shooting with an on camera flash so I won''t go on about it.<br/> <br/>The composition is ok, nothing spectacular.  I think this image could have been improved with a lower camera angle.  Right before you "surprised" her, you could have knelt (is that a word?) down to her level.  That way we''re getting a little different point of view as opposed to a "normal" point of view.  To me, things like that add a different dimension to an image.<br/> <br/>I hope this helps,<br/>Quadrajet</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club. Well, I have to say that this shot does have that "surprise" look to it.  It makes me wish you had included some information in the comments eluding to what she was doing to look so surprised. :) I think some commenters are confusing focus with motion blur.  The part of the exposure that was caught by the flash is certainly in focus.  What they''re referring to is the motion blur caused by a combination of ambient light and a longer shutter speed.  At any rate, I think this movement helps emphasize the element of "surprise" in that, thanks to the motion blur, we can see that she''s actually in the process of turning around/being caught. People have mentioned the red eye, and that is an unfortunate side effect of shooting with an on camera flash so I won''t go on about it. The composition is ok, nothing spectacular.  I think this image could have been improved with a lower camera angle.  Right before you "surprised" her, you could have knelt (is that a word?) down to her level.  That way we''re getting a little different point of view as opposed to a "normal" point of view.  To me, things like that add a different dimension to an image. I hope this helps,Quadrajet', '2003-12-04 23:54:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(504253, 47791, 7687, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>I have to tell you...when I first saw this shot I had it pegged (in my mind) for a ribbon...unfortunately you didn''t get one.<br/> <br/>I noticed you had mixed comments regarding the specular highlights around the pin.  I think it helps emphasize the fact that you''re actually PRESSING the pin into the balloon...I think it''s an excellent touch.<br/> <br/>I like the different colored balloons and I feel the yellow balloon was the right choice to have up front.  <br/> <br/>As far as the shallow DOF goes, I like it...it makes the pin/yellow balloon the focus of the image rather than something secondary.<br/> <br/>Nice shot, I''m sorry it didn''t finish higher!<br/>Quadrajet</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club. I have to tell you...when I first saw this shot I had it pegged (in my mind) for a ribbon...unfortunately you didn''t get one. I noticed you had mixed comments regarding the specular highlights around the pin.  I think it helps emphasize the fact that you''re actually PRESSING the pin into the balloon...I think it''s an excellent touch. I like the different colored balloons and I feel the yellow balloon was the right choice to have up front.   As far as the shallow DOF goes, I like it...it makes the pin/yellow balloon the focus of the image rather than something secondary. Nice shot, I''m sorry it didn''t finish higher!Quadrajet', '2003-12-05 00:14:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(504447, 48144, 3585, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Time<br/> <br/>Nice genuine capture of a surprised look, probably the only one in this challenge.<br/>(I wonder why a crowd so intense on the topic of challenge conformity scored you so low)<br/>Subject probably a little too central, moved too the right a bit would strengthen the composition. I personally would have zoomed in to keep just the eyes and mouth in the frame or zoomed out to have the full face featured.<br/> <br/>The white patch behind her shoulder could now be easily cloned out. To avoid having to do this you could have chosen a lower angle for your shot.<br/> <br/>Hope this helps, JJ<br/></td>', 'Critique Club Time Nice genuine capture of a surprised look, probably the only one in this challenge.(I wonder why a crowd so intense on the topic of challenge conformity scored you so low)Subject probably a little too central, moved too the right a bit would strengthen the composition. I personally would have zoomed in to keep just the eyes and mouth in the frame or zoomed out to have the full face featured. The white patch behind her shoulder could now be easily cloned out. To avoid having to do this you could have chosen a lower angle for your shot. Hope this helps, JJ', '2003-12-05 09:24:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(504543, 48265, 7969, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the CC, obviously a loved shot and a deserved ribbon winner.  I think you did very well to get the mouse to stay in that egg shell first of all.  Of course it is a good shot as is, but here is my opinion what perhaps could improve it.  The egg holder could possibly be black (or white) to give a more monochromatic color scheme matching the mouse or perhaps even cropped out, I find the large blue section a  little distracting, then again you could try it in b&amp;w also. The mouse could also be facing the camera, this I think would be the biggest improvement possible. Perhaps cropping a little more on the right moving the subject a bit more into that corner would make him seem like he has more room on the left to ''look into''.   These are just some thoughts, to give something to think about, very nice job and congrats on the ribbon!! Well done!</td>', 'Greetings from the CC, obviously a loved shot and a deserved ribbon winner.  I think you did very well to get the mouse to stay in that egg shell first of all.  Of course it is a good shot as is, but here is my opinion what perhaps could improve it.  The egg holder could possibly be black (or white) to give a more monochromatic color scheme matching the mouse or perhaps even cropped out, I find the large blue section a  little distracting, then again you could try it in b&w also. The mouse could also be facing the camera, this I think would be the biggest improvement possible. Perhaps cropping a little more on the right moving the subject a bit more into that corner would make him seem like he has more room on the left to ''look into''.   These are just some thoughts, to give something to think about, very nice job and congrats on the ribbon!! Well done!', '2003-12-05 11:27:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(504814, 48163, 14623, '<td valign="top">Thats a nice shot! Great eyes... Have you tried suspending the butterfly in mid-air using a black thread?</td>', 'Thats a nice shot! Great eyes... Have you tried suspending the butterfly in mid-air using a black thread?', '2003-12-05 15:43:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(504908, 48207, 10402, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>I think I see the Indian head: the long vine that crosses the window is the top of his head, the vines to the left are feathers, and the shorter vine below is the top of his ear. Right? It''s fun that you saw it and made it into an image, and I confess that it was fun when I solved your puzzle (although I wasn''t particularly surprised, probably because unlike you, I knew something must be there). If that''s what you wanted to accomplish with this photo, you succeeded!<br/> <br/>This image is loaded with lots of interesting colors and textures: leaves, smoke, grass, and several textures of wood to name a few. And the technical things like focus, exposure, contrast, and sharpness are great. But there is no center of interest; nothing grabs the viewer''s attention and entices him to stay and look around except the hope of finding a hidden Indian. And he is very subtly hidden indeed.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! I think I see the Indian head: the long vine that crosses the window is the top of his head, the vines to the left are feathers, and the shorter vine below is the top of his ear. Right? It''s fun that you saw it and made it into an image, and I confess that it was fun when I solved your puzzle (although I wasn''t particularly surprised, probably because unlike you, I knew something must be there). If that''s what you wanted to accomplish with this photo, you succeeded! This image is loaded with lots of interesting colors and textures: leaves, smoke, grass, and several textures of wood to name a few. And the technical things like focus, exposure, contrast, and sharpness are great. But there is no center of interest; nothing grabs the viewer''s attention and entices him to stay and look around except the hope of finding a hidden Indian. And he is very subtly hidden indeed.', '2003-12-05 18:10:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(504991, 48117, 14787, '<td valign="top">hahahaha funny. the blurriness around the hair is a bit bothersome, but everything else is very sharp.</td>', 'hahahaha funny. the blurriness around the hair is a bit bothersome, but everything else is very sharp.', '2003-12-05 20:09:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(505038, 48199, 12611, '<td valign="top">nice image but- he knew this was coming- his shirt is soaking wet and the water didn''t land yet.... must have been one or two throws before this one?</td>', 'nice image but- he knew this was coming- his shirt is soaking wet and the water didn''t land yet.... must have been one or two throws before this one?', '2003-12-05 22:01:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(505759, 48250, 14001, '<td valign="top">Really entered this pic to prove a point about the Propaganda challenge.  It''s not really a very good capture, really just a high quality snap-shot, yet it almost scored as well my Progranda image!<br/> <br/>But it''s straight forward.  Nothing subtle here, bright colors, and meets the challange very well.<br/> <br/>It''s really not that I think this photo deserved worse, it''s just that I think "Road Signs Never Lie" deserved so much better.  This entry really shows that Road Signs was in fact "hammered" by voters thinking that it didn''t meet "their" criteria of what propaganda is and in that case, isn''t.<br/> <br/>Anyway, I proved my point ( I think), so I''m finally moving on.</td>', 'Really entered this pic to prove a point about the Propaganda challenge.  It''s not really a very good capture, really just a high quality snap-shot, yet it almost scored as well my Progranda image! But it''s straight forward.  Nothing subtle here, bright colors, and meets the challange very well. It''s really not that I think this photo deserved worse, it''s just that I think "Road Signs Never Lie" deserved so much better.  This entry really shows that Road Signs was in fact "hammered" by voters thinking that it didn''t meet "their" criteria of what propaganda is and in that case, isn''t. Anyway, I proved my point ( I think), so I''m finally moving on.', '2003-12-07 02:32:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(505819, 48240, 9906, '<td valign="top">From the Critique Club<br/> <br/>Things I like:  Lighting is pretty nice here, but there is a touch of shadow (from the flash?) that doesn''t look right.  With such a light colored background you don''t have hot spots which is good.  Very basic color scheme.  You have basically two colors here and that works nicely though colors ARE a little dull.  Very cute model!  I know how much fun it can be working with kids.  Nice cropping to help tell the story without too many unneeded elements!<br/> <br/>Things I don''t like:  This shot is a touch grainy or noisy.  This is probably from the ISO setting.  I have a camera similar to yours (without ALL that zoom which I want) and try to take all my shots at ISO 100.  Colors like I said are a little dull.  You can pull these out with levels, or hue/saturation.  The alligator could use a little more light to help bring out some details.  I am still trying to work out my own lighting issues so I can''t give specific tips here.  I also notice that your camera (like mine, ugh) has perspective issues because of the lenses used.  The only way I know to help with this is always try and shoot as level as possible.  Oh and your model looks like she is having a ball and doesn''t really look all that surprised.<br/> <br/>Overall a cute shot with a fun concept.  If you had time to play around with it more I''m sure you could have turned it into a great one!<br/>TC</td>', 'From the Critique Club Things I like:  Lighting is pretty nice here, but there is a touch of shadow (from the flash?) that doesn''t look right.  With such a light colored background you don''t have hot spots which is good.  Very basic color scheme.  You have basically two colors here and that works nicely though colors ARE a little dull.  Very cute model!  I know how much fun it can be working with kids.  Nice cropping to help tell the story without too many unneeded elements! Things I don''t like:  This shot is a touch grainy or noisy.  This is probably from the ISO setting.  I have a camera similar to yours (without ALL that zoom which I want) and try to take all my shots at ISO 100.  Colors like I said are a little dull.  You can pull these out with levels, or hue/saturation.  The alligator could use a little more light to help bring out some details.  I am still trying to work out my own lighting issues so I can''t give specific tips here.  I also notice that your camera (like mine, ugh) has perspective issues because of the lenses used.  The only way I know to help with this is always try and shoot as level as possible.  Oh and your model looks like she is having a ball and doesn''t really look all that surprised. Overall a cute shot with a fun concept.  If you had time to play around with it more I''m sure you could have turned it into a great one!TC', '2003-12-07 10:06:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(505892, 48046, 3585, '<td valign="top">Sunday Night Critique Club Special<br/> <br/>Altogether a good and fun picture with surprisingly good lighting for a snapshot. <br/>Composition wise a few flaws: To bad your subject is positioned dead center; putting your model on the left of the frame would allow more of the toilet to bee seen (I did not notice what your son was actually doing the first time I saw this image) and would strengthen the composition. Also avoidable: the scalping of your progeny shows in what haste you took that one but does not improve the overall appearance of the picture. <br/>This was a near great picture. I know it is hard to think of all those things and also succeed at clicking at the right time.<br/> <br/>I have always been amazed at all the parameters seasoned photojournalism pros can think of in a split second when they are capturing in style an event in motion.<br/> <br/>All the best, JJ <br/></td>', 'Sunday Night Critique Club Special Altogether a good and fun picture with surprisingly good lighting for a snapshot. Composition wise a few flaws: To bad your subject is positioned dead center; putting your model on the left of the frame would allow more of the toilet to bee seen (I did not notice what your son was actually doing the first time I saw this image) and would strengthen the composition. Also avoidable: the scalping of your progeny shows in what haste you took that one but does not improve the overall appearance of the picture. This was a near great picture. I know it is hard to think of all those things and also succeed at clicking at the right time. I have always been amazed at all the parameters seasoned photojournalism pros can think of in a split second when they are capturing in style an event in motion. All the best, JJ ', '2003-12-07 13:57:19.000000', NULL, 0);
